#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046665} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407350} :activity/id #uuid "f13d4530-1c8e-598a-986b-9b8f2a13e396" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050164 :topic/id #uuid "32af04f7-8bd5-5a90-8e7c-6c111c983ee8" :topic/math-academy-id 1955} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407349 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407348 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f49be2f-9fbd-57d9-8743-c7f792e14929" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ z=-y+1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407337} {:answer/id #uuid "40205211-9c82-5bbc-b29c-5211d1a45bbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ z=-2y-1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407338} {:answer/id #uuid "645649e7-f6ca-5dcf-a129-028646be2cfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ z=y-1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407340} {:answer/id #uuid "8d3f6706-e60e-5d15-b360-58385706ee4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ z=-2y+1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407341} {:answer/id #uuid "d4018ef0-2dba-5e3f-8527-3ea960bf4de4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ z=-y-1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407343}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407343} :answer-field/id #uuid "e94262ee-f4dd-5ac5-a015-9c5cf4798aa0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407347}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "05b78370-0b37-5800-aa28-6b6b17964b90" :question/math-academy-id "q-71216" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=1.$ Given that the point $P(1,0,-1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=x{e}^{xy}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(1,0,-1)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(1,0)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}$ at $(1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,0) & =1\\cdot {e}^{1\\cdot 0}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(1,0)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(1,0)}(y-0) \\\\ z-(-1) & =(-1)y \\\\ z & =-y-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,0,-1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ z=-y-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8482a7f1-9b85-58c1-b946-6953c47668a6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407351}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407350 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125189 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25cf98fa-a5b3-5e6b-b710-84fa1db64ec9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "j-3\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407336} {:answer/id #uuid "5de2add0-6423-5754-a07f-96730ef51cc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "-j-2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407339} {:answer/id #uuid "bccf173f-8570-55d3-b189-673a90a1c377" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "3j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407342} {:answer/id #uuid "db135884-f54c-58f1-b6d6-52b5e9137f78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "2j+3\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407344} {:answer/id #uuid "fd44ef3e-d356-53fe-b14e-c4931ae0f4a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "-2j+\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407345}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407336} :answer-field/id #uuid "a315569d-b4ce-597c-a11e-e51ae4013a54" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407346}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ecca0679-247f-506b-95a3-b2be27b80569" :question/math-academy-id "q-64972" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=1.$ Given that the point $P(1,-1,2)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}={x}^{2}(2xy-1)$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(1,-1).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,-1) & =(1)^{2}(2(1)(-1)-1)=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(1,-1) & ={f}_{y}(1,-1)(y+1) \\\\ z-2 & =-3(y+1) \\\\ z-2 & =-3y-3 \\\\ z & =-3y-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,-1,2)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ z=-3y-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=1,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=-3y-1$ (or $-3y-z-1=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,-3,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,-3,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =j-3\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $j-3\\,k.$"} :step/id #uuid "e1916473-66ad-5022-aafa-9787da440dac" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407349}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407351 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338923 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "156f0e60-a655-5cee-ace2-00f94feeb9b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=x-2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336240} {:answer/id #uuid "3998953b-84b0-5889-81f7-4dc1224db93d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=x+2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336579} {:answer/id #uuid "4603346d-02b6-5575-9939-64549e4f33fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336687} {:answer/id #uuid "6b358259-0538-5cb3-840e-3b6005519fca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=-x+2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336987} {:answer/id #uuid "ef59035a-1a91-53fe-8a72-a5d6bc5d3dc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=-2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338133}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336579} :answer-field/id #uuid "67c2ebb0-7f04-5390-9b3f-6c5b9ac02ee1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338464}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ceda561f-8269-5197-b05a-efc41af9a9a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-71218" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=1.$ Given that the point $P(0,1,2)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(0,1,2)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(0,1)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(0,1)} & =(0)^{2}+(1)^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0+1) \\\\  & =1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1 \\\\  & =1-0 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(0,1)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(0,1)}(x-0) \\\\ z-2 & =1(x) \\\\ z & =x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(0,1,2)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f2c84ac0-3b38-584c-9809-dfa6181b9351"}] :activity/title "Geometric Interpretations of Partial Derivatives" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407335}]}
